WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.077
A module is a Python file that contains code you can use in another file or notebook.

00:00:05.077 --> 00:00:09.722
Python already comes with many built-in modules.

00:00:09.722 --> 00:00:14.847
For example, the math module contains mathematical tools such as square root and pi.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:18.917
If we write import math, Python loads that module.

00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:24.692
Then we can use tools inside it, such as math.sqrt or math.pi.

00:00:24.692 --> 00:00:28.852
A helpful analogy is to think of a module as a toolbox.

00:00:28.852 --> 00:00:34.922
The module is the toolbox, and the functions or values inside the module are the tools.

00:00:34.922 --> 00:00:39.562
When you import the toolbox, you can use the tools inside it.

00:00:39.562 --> 00:00:42.982
One important detail is the dot notation.

00:00:42.982 --> 00:00:47.762
When we write math.sqrt, we are saying: go into the math module and use the sqrt function stored

00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:50.552
there. This helps keep names organized.

00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:59.102
If every function from every module automatically appeared in our program without a module name,

00:00:59.102 --> 00:01:02.532
it would become difficult to know where things came from.

00:01:02.532 --> 00:01:07.232
So a module helps with reuse, but it also helps with clarity.

00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:10.400
It tells us where a function or value belongs.
